<HowToGuide title="Key Elements: Myth, Archetype, Minimalist Language, Repetition" currentGuide="key-elements">
  <p>
    The play begins with a myth that stays in the mind &mdash; a story as old as human history,
    whose characters and plot carry an encoded message down the centuries to us, the scriptwriters.
    It is our task to present the story without losing the message.
  </p>
  <p>
    We follow the storyline. We keep the same characters. We do not change details. We believe the
    children of today are no less able to recognise and understand the physical, psychological, and
    emotional difficulties of life. We believe children still need these ancestral messages on how
    to survive, cope, and thrive.
  </p>
  <p>
    What we do with the material is to elongate it &mdash; both with language and with action. We
    reach inside the myth and find the archetypes of story, situation, and character, and we amplify
    them so they come alive as fully as possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because we believe each line of text carries great weight, scripts may be only seven pages of
    actual text. These few lines will become a production lasting 40&ndash;50 minutes. The script
    contains the myth, the archetypes, and as much of the culture of origin as possible.
  </p>

  <h2>Myth</h2>
  <p>
    A myth is a story told and retold until it embodies the great truths of the people telling it.
  </p>
  <p>
    By choosing myths for the basis of our scripts, we work solidly within the archetypal framework.
    Once we understood that we were tapping into a reservoir of human knowledge, we began to
    understand why we received the same attention and enthusiasm for each production regardless of
    where it was set, the plot or characters, or what actions went on during the play. The branches
    are as wide and variable as the world; the root is in one source.
  </p>
  <p>
    When we decide to dramatise a myth, we choose it for one of three reasons: we&rsquo;ve been
    commissioned to do it, the message it contains is important to the culture, or the message is
    important to one of us. We look up all the versions we can find, including translations and
    early texts, and create a storyboard from the elements we want to fit together.
  </p>
  <p>
    The next step is to put the script into the Players&rsquo; hands. Sometimes as early as the
    first rehearsal there are changes needed; sometimes the changes occur on tour as audiences ask
    questions or make comments that point to a necessary evolution.
  </p>

  <h2>Archetype</h2>
  <p>
    The idea of archetypes re-entered Western thought through Carl Jung. Each of us holds in our
    unconscious a pool of images, a reservoir of archetypes. Myths and mythic images re-enter
    consciousness if given the right trigger.
  </p>
  <p>
    Jung teaches us that the archetypes are carried inside us, like pieces of DNA. They are flat,
    or un-constituted, until activated. Once activated, they inflate and become part of a
    person&rsquo;s active psychological makeup. Among the first is &ldquo;mother&rdquo; and
    &ldquo;father&rdquo;: all children, no matter how abused, innately understand what a good
    mother&rsquo;s or father&rsquo;s behaviours would be.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is the scriptwriter&rsquo;s job to provide the triggers that activate the archetypal
    imagination &mdash; that pull the archetype from the collective unconscious into life. In
    <em>One-Inch-Fellow</em>, for example, the children meet a mother and father, a one-inch boy,
    a samurai, a fool, a Keeper, and a dragon. Each of those characters is an archetype. Each
    archetype reacts to the others and becomes one of the company &mdash; this is both the story
    and the psychological lesson.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because we are very conscious of this necessity for goodness in our work, we do not put
    knowingly any image or action in the plays that can trigger a negative archetype or model a
    negative behaviour. If the story has a witch or a magician, they are included but &mdash; as
    in the original tale &mdash; receive their just outcome.
  </p>

  <h2>Minimalist language</h2>
  <p>
    When the visual orientation of modern audiences is turned over to their imagination, the
    auditory orientation of the early oral culture replaces it. The language needed to be sparse,
    minimal. There are not many words, and each word is chosen for the information or image it
    conveys.
  </p>
  <p>
    When there is dialogue, each line is precise and very clear in meaning: &ldquo;I am going over
    the mountain.&rdquo; &ldquo;This is my son, Mitsu. He winds bobbins for me. His bobbins are
    wound better than any other bobbins in Japan.&rdquo; This sparsity of words allows a tempo of
    delivery that suggests &ldquo;something special&rdquo; &mdash; it is not conversational.
  </p>
  <p>
    With sparsity, precision, and clarity, there is no mistaking the plot, the characters, the
    action, and the language. Even Kindergarteners understand what they are experiencing. The more
    cluttered the language, the more cluttered the work.
  </p>
  <p>The language is &ldquo;poem-like&rdquo; or &ldquo;haiku-like&rdquo;:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It is not discursive or narrative in style.</li>
    <li>It is precise; each line is absolutely clear in meaning.</li>
    <li>There are no conversational, referring pronouns.</li>
    <li>Key plot shifts are repeated.</li>
    <li>Important ideas are repeated.</li>
    <li>Everyone can conceptualise what is happening &mdash; from Kindergarten to adult.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Repetition</h2>
  <p>
    Repetition is used in scripts to emphasise. When a new character is introduced, the
    script has the character&rsquo;s name and qualities repeated by various Players. The main theme
    of the play or a scene may be repeated several times, often using different words emphasising
    the concept.
  </p>
  <p>
    Repetition takes many forms:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <em>Scene repeats:</em> there are three children, so three scenes where the first two times
      the characters fail but the third child succeeds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Theme repeats:</em> various characters refer to the goal or other main point.
    </li>
    <li><em>Line repeats,</em> including songs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The ear catches the pattern; the mind knows what&rsquo;s coming; the release when the pattern
    is finally broken is the play&rsquo;s pivot. Repetition is not filler &mdash; it is the
    architecture that makes surprise possible.
  </p>

  <h2>The exotic and the unfamiliar</h2>
  <p>
    One goal of using exotic settings is to expand children&rsquo;s awareness of different cultures
    of the world and to broaden their understanding. &ldquo;Exotic&rdquo; means different things to
    different people &mdash; for city children, a play set in rural times or places is exotic. For
    rural children, a city setting is exotic. The unfamiliar time and place are not frightening,
    because the children, in &ldquo;supplying&rdquo; the props through their imaginations, use
    familiar objects as a base.
  </p>
  <p>
    Even as simple a story as <em>The Golden Goose</em> is exotic: there is a little magic man, it
    is set in the country, the characters cut wood to provide a living, it is set in a peasant
    world. Strange and exotic invites curiosity and awakens the mind.
  </p>
</HowToGuide>
